WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.102
Day 6 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.652 --> 00:00:09.763
Synthetic test data with LLMs: realistic, valid and PII-free.

00:00:10.313 --> 00:00:15.174
A test dataset combining Faker for volume and an LLM for edge cases, validated

00:00:15.174 --> 00:00:17.978
against a schema and with no production data.

00:00:19.278 --> 00:00:22.380
Test data is one of the oldest bottlenecks in QA.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.891
Either you use a copy of production, with all the legal risk that implies, or you

00:00:27.891 --> 00:00:33.230
use data so tidy it never triggers a bug: names without accents, perfect addresses,

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.131
round amounts.

00:00:34.431 --> 00:00:38.926
Real defects show up with the surname that has an apostrophe, the email with a

00:00:38.926 --> 00:00:42.038
subdomain or the note someone filled in with an emoji.

00:00:43.338 --> 00:00:47.729
An LLM is good at exactly that: producing realistic variety.

00:00:48.029 --> 00:00:51.468
But it also produces invalid rows with great confidence.

00:00:51.768 --> 00:00:56.255
This guide combines three pieces to keep the best of each: a schema that defines

00:00:56.255 --> 00:01:00.967
what is valid, Faker for volume and the model only for the odd cases, with automatic

00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:02.313
validation of every row.

00:01:03.613 --> 00:01:05.310
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:07.662
The qa-ai-lab repo.

00:01:08.213 --> 00:01:10.038
A local model with Ollama.

00:01:11.338 --> 00:01:12.618
This is the setup.

00:01:12.918 --> 00:01:17.135
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:18.435 --> 00:01:20.701
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:22.001 --> 00:01:24.268
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:25.568 --> 00:01:26.604
Step 1.

00:01:26.904 --> 00:01:28.381
The schema rules.

00:01:28.931 --> 00:01:30.605
Define the schema first.

00:01:30.905 --> 00:01:33.880
It's the source of truth for what valid data is.

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:37.382
Write the schema before generating a single row.

00:01:37.682 --> 00:01:42.513
It's the contract for your data: which fields exist, what type they are, which

00:01:42.513 --> 00:01:45.115
ranges they accept and which are required.

00:01:45.415 --> 00:01:50.251
With Zod or JSON Schema you express it once and use it in three places: to validate

00:01:50.251 --> 00:01:54.970
what Faker produces, to validate what the model produces and as documentation for

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:55.495
the team.

00:01:55.795 --> 00:02:00.265
If the schema is loose, for example it accepts any string as a date, invalid data

00:02:00.265 --> 00:02:04.459
slips through and you'll discover it in a test failing for the wrong reason.

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:06.957
Step 2.

00:02:07.257 --> 00:02:08.618
Faker for volume.

00:02:09.168 --> 00:02:13.838
Use Faker with a fixed seed for volume: fast, free and reproducible.

00:02:14.388 --> 00:02:17.130
For the thousands of "normal" rows you don't need a model.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:21.682
Faker generates them in milliseconds, at no cost and with no network.

00:02:21.982 --> 00:02:26.885
What matters is pinning the seed: with the same seed you get exactly the same data

00:02:26.885 --> 00:02:29.636
on your machine, on your teammate's and in CI.

00:02:29.936 --> 00:02:33.584
That turns an intermittent failure into a reproducible one.

00:02:33.884 --> 00:02:38.739
Also choose the locale matching your users, because it changes the format of names,

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:43.477
phone numbers and addresses, and those formats are part of what you want to test.

00:02:44.777 --> 00:02:45.859
Step 3.

00:02:46.159 --> 00:02:48.042
The model for the odd cases.

00:02:48.592 --> 00:02:53.451
Use the LLM for what Faker can't give: realistic edge cases (apostrophes in

00:02:53.451 --> 00:02:58.568
surnames, accented names, emails with subdomains, notes with emojis or quotes).

00:02:59.118 --> 00:03:03.610
Ask the model for what a library can't invent: believable variants that break

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:04.310
assumptions.

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:06.517
Be specific in the request.

00:03:06.817 --> 00:03:11.617
Instead of "generate weird data", list the categories you care about: compound

00:03:11.617 --> 00:03:16.725
surnames and ones with apostrophes, single-letter names, emails with subdomains and

00:03:16.725 --> 00:03:21.648
plus signs, free text with quotes, line breaks or emojis, dates at the edge of a

00:03:21.648 --> 00:03:22.017
range.

00:03:22.317 --> 00:03:24.688
Ask for a few rows per category.

00:03:24.988 --> 00:03:29.283
Ten well-thought-out cases are worth more than five hundred similar ones, and they

00:03:29.283 --> 00:03:30.436
are cheaper to review.

00:03:31.736 --> 00:03:32.749
Step 4.

00:03:33.049 --> 00:03:34.711
Row-by-row validation.

00:03:35.261 --> 00:03:39.780
Ask the model for JSON output and validate every row against the schema.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.788
Whatever fails gets discarded and counted.

00:03:43.338 --> 00:03:48.136
Ask for JSON output and pass every row through the schema before accepting it.

00:03:48.436 --> 00:03:51.561
Whatever doesn't validate gets discarded and counted.

00:03:51.861 --> 00:03:56.392
That counter is a useful metric: if the model fails one row in four, the prompt

00:03:56.392 --> 00:04:00.235
needs a format example or the schema has a rule you didn't explain.

00:04:00.535 --> 00:04:05.042
Don't fix invalid rows by hand, because that breaks reproducibility.

00:04:05.342 --> 00:04:11.735
Don't trust a visual review either: a date such as 2026-02-30 looks perfect and

00:04:11.735 --> 00:04:12.868
doesn't exist.

00:04:14.168 --> 00:04:15.297
Step 5.

00:04:15.597 --> 00:04:17.561
Production stays out of the prompt.

00:04:18.112 --> 00:04:20.657
Never paste production data into the prompt.

00:04:20.957 --> 00:04:23.757
Describe the shape of the data, not the data.

00:04:24.307 --> 00:04:29.098
The rule is simple and has no exceptions: the prompt carries the shape of the data,

00:04:29.098 --> 00:04:29.964
never the data.

00:04:30.264 --> 00:04:35.423
Describe fields, formats and oddities you want to see, without pasting real records,

00:04:35.423 --> 00:04:40.336
not even "hand-anonymized" ones, because that anonymization almost always leaves

00:04:40.336 --> 00:04:41.749
something identifiable.

00:04:42.049 --> 00:04:46.893
If you need synthetic data to resemble the real data statistically, describe the

00:04:46.893 --> 00:04:48.286
distributions in words.

00:04:48.586 --> 00:04:53.840
And verify the result: check that no names or ID numbers match real people in your

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:54.417
database.

00:04:55.717 --> 00:04:56.904
Step 6.

00:04:57.204 --> 00:04:59.714
Seed, prompt and dataset versioned.

00:05:00.264 --> 00:05:04.238
Version the seed, the prompt and the final dataset in data/.

00:05:04.538 --> 00:05:07.071
A dataset you can't regenerate is debt.

00:05:07.621 --> 00:05:12.368
Store in data/ the three things that let you regenerate the set: the Faker seed, the

00:05:12.368 --> 00:05:16.041
prompt used with the model and the final, already validated file.

00:05:16.341 --> 00:05:20.805
The part generated by the model isn't reproducible bit for bit, which is why you

00:05:20.805 --> 00:05:23.205
version the result and not just the recipe.

00:05:23.505 --> 00:05:25.598
Also note the model and the date.

00:05:25.898 --> 00:05:30.271
When a test fails six months from now, you'll be able to tell which data it ran

00:05:30.271 --> 00:05:34.201
with, and if the set needs extending you'll start from something known.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.607
An example.

00:05:37.157 --> 00:05:39.702
A team tests a customer sign-up form.

00:05:40.002 --> 00:05:44.196
With Faker and a fixed seed they generate a thousand valid customers in under a

00:05:44.196 --> 00:05:44.567
second.

00:05:44.867 --> 00:05:49.026
Then they ask the model for twenty odd cases across five categories.

00:05:49.326 --> 00:05:54.284
Of the twenty rows, the schema rejects four: two impossible birth dates, a phone

00:05:54.284 --> 00:05:57.259
number with letters and an email with no domain.

00:05:57.559 --> 00:05:58.827
Sixteen remain.

00:05:59.127 --> 00:06:03.889
Running the suite with those sixteen, two tests fail: the surname O'Connor breaks a

00:06:03.889 --> 00:06:07.732
badly escaped query and a name with an emoji gets stored truncated.

00:06:08.032 --> 00:06:11.472
None of the thousand tidy records had found those two defects.

00:06:12.772 --> 00:06:14.435
The most common mistakes.

00:06:14.985 --> 00:06:19.573
Generating everything with the model, even the volume Faker handles for free.

00:06:20.123 --> 00:06:24.619
Accepting rows by how they look, without validating them against the schema.

00:06:25.169 --> 00:06:28.132
Pasting a real record "as a format example".

00:06:28.682 --> 00:06:32.458
Not saving the final dataset and being unable to repeat a run.

00:06:33.758 --> 00:06:36.001
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:36.551 --> 00:06:40.199
The schema rejects invalid dates, emails and ranges.

00:06:40.749 --> 00:06:44.467
Faker runs with a fixed seed and gives the same result twice.

00:06:45.017 --> 00:06:48.851
Every model row went through the schema and there is a discard count.

00:06:49.401 --> 00:06:53.525
Seed, prompt, model, date and dataset are in data/.

00:06:54.825 --> 00:06:56.720
And where AI fell short.

00:06:57.271 --> 00:07:01.476
It returns JSON that looks valid and isn't (dates, formats).

00:07:01.776 --> 00:07:05.389
Without automatic validation, those errors reach your tests.

00:07:06.689 --> 00:07:08.178
That was day 6.

00:07:08.478 --> 00:07:13.452
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:13.452 --> 00:07:14.460
asked questions.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:21.502
Continue with day 7: Harnesses 101: test harness, eval harness and agent harness.
